\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-d3, Q: d1-d4, Q: d1-d5, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, P: d6-c7, P: d6-e7, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in the early-to-mid game phase. My king is safe on e1, and all major pieces except the d-pawn and g-pawn have not moved. Black has advanced several pawns, with a knight on c6 (NBF1), bishop on g7 (BBF1), and a strong queen on d8. Notably, Black's pawn on e5 and my pawn on e4 are locked in opposition. The black knight on c6 exerts pressure on my d4 square, and there’s a potential threat along the long diagonal if my light-squared bishop remains blocked.\n\nI have a central pawn tension between e4 (PWF1) and e5 (PBF1). Black just played ...d6–c5 or ...e7–e5 recently (given en passant status), but no immediate threats exist. My kingside is solid, and castling is still possible via O-O since both king and rook haven't moved and the path is clear.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: e4-e5 – Not available in the move list; otherwise would be a strong break.\n- Q: d1-d4 – Centralizes queen, eyeing e5 and supporting e4. Good control, but slightly premature without development.\n- B: f1-e2 – Develops the bishop, prepares kingside castling, keeps options open. Solid.\n- N: b1-c3 – Strong developing move, supports d5/e4, defends queen, natural tempo.\n- N: b1-a3 – Less ideal; knight is awkwardly placed, may reroute later.\n- P: b2-b4 – Commits to queenside space, but weakens c4 and exposes a4/a3.\n- B: c1-g5 – Possible pin on knight, but Nf6 isn’t played yet; currently ineffective.\n- K: e1-d2 – King in center? Too dangerous and wastes tempo.\n- R: h1-g1 – Prepares for kingside castling indirectly, but unusual to do before king moves.\n- P: d6-c7 / d6-e7 – These are Black’s pawn moves, not mine — invalid.\n\nBest candidates:\n1. **N: b1-c3** – Develops knight, supports center, enables castling, protects queen.\n2. **B: f1-e2** – Natural development, prepares K-O-O, connects rooks eventually.\n3. **Q: d1-d3** or **Q: d1-d4** – Active queen, but risks being harassed by minor pieces.\n\nReason: The most principled plan is rapid development and king safety. Nc3 is a cornerstone of many openings (like Queen’s Gambit or English), harmonizing with overall strategy. It doesn’t block any pawns, improves piece activity, and maintains flexibility. Be2 is also good, but Nc3 takes priority in central control when facing a symmetrical pawn structure.\n\nAnswer: N: b1-c3"}
