12_23_15_51_12	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|    |BBF1|    |KBF1|    |    |    | 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|    |    |    |    |    |BBF1|    | 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |NBF1|    |    |BWF1|BWF1|    |    |RBF1| 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |PBF1|    |    |PWF1|    |    |PBF1| 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |PBF1|    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|    |RWF1|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    |    |KWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, R: b1-a1, R: b1-c1, R: b1-d1, R: b1-e1, R: b1-f1, R: b1-g1, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, B: d6-c5, B: d6-b4, B: d6-a3, B: d6-c7, B: d6-b8, B: d6-e7, B: d6-f8, R: e2-d2, R: e2-e1, R: e2-e3, R: e2-e4, B: e6-d5, B: e6-c4, B: e6-b3, B: e6-f5, B: e6-g4, B: e6-h3, B: e6-d7, B: e6-c8, B: e6-f7, B: e6-g8, P: f2-f4, P: f2-g3, P: f2-f3, K: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-g3, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and it's my first move. The board shows that Black has already developed some minor pieces and moved pawns toward the center. My king is still in the corner (h1), and my kingside rook on e2 is somewhat exposed. Several of my pawns are on their starting ranks (a2, b2, c2, f2, g2, h2), meaning they can advance one or two squares. Notably, there is a white bishop on d6 — which is unusual for this early stage — suggesting this is not an opening from standard theory, possibly a gambit or middlegame position reached via irregular play. My light-squared bishop on e6 and dark-squared bishop on d6 are both active, eyeing central and queenside targets.\n\nBlack’s king is on e8, relatively uncastled or at least not safely tucked away, and their rooks are on a8 and h6 — the latter oddly advanced. Black also has a knight on a6 and bishops on c8 and g7. There seems to be an opportunity to exploit the lack of king safety.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: a2-a4 / b2-b4 / c2-c4 / f2-f4 / h2-h4**: Pawn pushes could aim for space or preparation, but advancing pawns without development or king safety is risky.\n- **R: b1-a1 / R: b1-c1 etc.**: Moving the rook from b1 does little immediately; no open files.\n- **B: d6-c5 / d6-b4 / d6-a3**: These target key central/light squares; Ba3 threatens the black queen if it were on d8, but currently no immediate material gain. Bb4 pins a knight if Nc6 exists — but here Black has Na6, so likely not relevant.\n- **B: d6-c7+?** – This would attack the black king directly! c7 is adjacent to e8, and if the bishop moves to c7, it checks the king: **B: d6-c7** gives check, potentially winning material or forcing disadvantageous moves.\n    - Is it legal? d6 to c7 is diagonal (d6→c7), yes.\n    - Is the path clear? d6 to c7: only one square, yes.\n    - Does it give check? c7 attacks b8, d8, and e8 — yes, e8 is where KBF1 (black king) is. So **B: d6-c7** delivers check.\n    - Can Black capture? Only if something defends c7 — currently, no black piece attacks c7. So unless Black moves king or blocks, it's a direct threat.\n    - However, wait: **is c7 occupied?** Looking at the board: c7 is empty. Yes. So **B: d6-c7 is a legal and strong move**, delivering check with tempo.\n\nEven stronger: **B: e6-g8**? That lands on g8 — but g8 is empty? No, g8 is empty according to the board. But g8 is where a black knight or king might be — but here, king is on e8, and g8 is empty. So **B: e6-g8** is possible? From e6 to g8 is diagonal (e6-f7-g8), but f7 is occupied? Let's see: f7 is empty (row 7: a7=pawn, g7=bishop). Wait, g7 has BBF1 — Black bishop on g7. So g8 is empty. But e6 to g8 passes through f7 — is f7 occupied?\n\nRow 7: f7 is empty (only a7 and g7 have black pieces). So yes, f7 is empty. So **B: e6-g8** is a valid move — and it captures nothing, but puts pressure? More importantly, **does it check the king?** g8 attacks h7, f7, e8 — yes! e8 again. So **B: e6-g8+** also gives check!\n\nBut wait: **which bishop should I move?**\n\nLet’s compare:\n\n- **B: d6-c7+**: Bishop from d6 to c7 → checks Ke8. After this, Black must respond: either Kb8, Kd8, or block with a piece (but no piece can interpose easily). Also, after ...Kb8, I can follow up with Rc1 or Qa4+, maybe winning.\n- **B: e6-g8+**: Bishop from e6 to g8 → checks Ke8. Then Black can play Kf8 or Kg8 or block? But g8 is now occupied by my bishop. This is also a check.\n\nBut here's the critical difference: **Is B: e6-g8 safe?** After Bxg8, Black plays Rxg8 — I lose a bishop for a rook? But my bishop was on e6, and I capture nothing — just deliver check and get recaptured. So it’s a waste.\n\nWhereas **B: d6-c7** — if Black cannot capture, then I have a bishop deep in their position, attacking heavily. Can Black capture on c7? Currently, no piece attacks c7. Their pieces:\n- a6 knight: doesn't reach c7\n- b7 pawn? No, b7 is empty\n- d7? Empty\nSo **no one can capture on c7 immediately**.\n\nThus, **B: d6-c7+** is a free check, and likely wins material next move (e.g., if king runs, I may win a rook or force mate).\n\nAlso, is the bishop pinned? d6 — is it defended? It's on d6, originally from c1 probably. Who defends it? Currently, only if another piece covers it — but no immediate attacker. e5 has a white pawn, so support from center.\n\nBut crucially: **after B: d6-c7+, Black must move the king**. Then I can follow up with heavy pieces.\n\nAlternatively, **B: d6-b4** pins a knight if Nc6 — but there is no Nc6. Black has Na6, so not relevant.\n\nTherefore, **B: d6-c7** stands out as a powerful, forcing, and aggressive move.\n\nReason: It delivers immediate check to the black king, exploiting poor king placement and lack of defenders around c7. It forces Black into a defensive posture, likely losing time or material. It increases pressure without risk, as the square is not currently under attack by any Black piece.\n\nAnswer: B: d6-c7"}
