ot diagonal. Diagonals from c7 are:\n\n- c7-b6-a5 (down-left)\n- c7-d6-e5-f4-g3-h2 (down-right)\n- c7-d8 (up-right)\n- c7-b8 (up-left)\n\ne7 is not on any of these. So bishop on c7 does **not** attack e7.\n\nAny other piece? Rook on b1 — b1-e1? No, no connection. Rook on e2 — blocked by own pawn on e5.\n\nSo black king on e7 is **not in check**, but it's incredibly exposed.\n\nNow, evaluating threats and opportunities.\n\nMy most urgent concern: develop pieces and exploit the exposed black king.\n\nLooking at the valid moves, I can:\n\n- Develop the rook on b1 — possibly to central files.\n- Push pawns in the center or flank.\n- Use the bishop on c7 — which is already deep in enemy territory — to reposition for attack.\n\nBut notice: the bishop on c7 (BWF1) has valid moves: B: c7-b6, c7-a5, c7-d6, c7-b8, c7-d8.\n\nAmong these, **c7-d6** stands out — d6 is one diagonal square away from e7. From d6, the bishop would attack e7 directly — delivering check!\n\nAnd after B:c7-d6+, the black king must move — possible retreats: e7-e8, e7-d7, e7-f7, e7-d8, e7-f8. But many of those may be covered or lead to further tactics.\n\nMoreover, d6 is currently empty — and no piece blocks the c7-d6 diagonal.\n\nSo **B: c7-d6** is a move that delivers check and exploits the king's exposure.\n\nIs it safe? Can Black capture the bishop on d6 after? From d6, the bishop can be captured by:\n\n- e5-pawn — but e5 has a **white** pawn — so no.\n- c7 is now empty — no recapture from behind.\n- Other pieces? Black has a knight on a6 (NBF1) — too far. Bishop on g7 (BBF1) — on g7, can it go to d6? g7-f6-e5-d4-c3-b2-a1 — no. g7-h6, f5, etc. — not reaching d6.\n\nSo no immediate capture of a bishop on d6.\n\nThus, B:c7-d6+ wins material or disrupts Black severely.\n\nAlternatively, pushing central pawns or developing rooks might be slower.\n\nAnother idea: can I trap the king? But without immediate mate, the goal is to maximize pressure.\n\nAlso, note that my rook on e2 is partially blocked by my o