, so the diagonal from c5 to e6 is open, and the square e6 is occupied by an enemy piece — that means **I can capture the black king if it were unprotected** — but of course, kings cannot be captured; instead, this means the king is **in check** if attacked.\n\nWait: Is the black king currently in check?\n\nFrom c5, the white bishop on c5 attacks d6, e7, f8 — but not e6. c5 to e6 is two files right and one rank up — that’s not diagonal. Diagonally from c5:  \n- c5 → d6 → e7 → f8 (a2-g8 diagonal)  \n- c5 → b4 → a3 (c5-a3 diagonal)\n\nSo e6 is **not** on the same diagonal as c5. c5 is light square (c-file odd, 5 odd → light), e6 is dark square (e odd, 6 even → dark). So bishop on c5 (light-squared) cannot reach e6 (dark). So no direct attack.\n\nBut wait — is there any check? Let\'s verify: none of my pieces are attacking e6. The rook on e2 is on e2, same file as e6 — yes! e2-e6 is four ranks up, all empty? Let\'s see:\n\nFile e: e2 (RWF1), e3 (empty), e4 (empty), e5 (PWF1 — white pawn!), e6 (KBF1 — black king). So e5 has a **white pawn**, blocking the rook’s path. So rook on e2 does **not** attack e6.\n\nSo black king is **not in check**.\n\nBut e5 has a white pawn (PWF1), which just moved — possibly from e4? But it\'s marked as "moved", not 2-step. Anyway, it\'s on e5.\n\nNow, key opportunity: **The black king is stranded on e6**, surrounded by limited protection. It has few escape squares: d6, d5, f5, f6, etc. Many are under control.\n\nMy bishop on c5 is ready to strike: it can go to b4, d4, e3, f4 (but f4 not listed), but valid moves include B: c5-b4, c5-a3, c5-d4, c5-e3, c5-b6, c5-a7, c5-d6, c5-e7, c5-f8.\n\nMost promising: **B: c5-e7** — this would place the bishop on e7, attacking f8 and importantly, **the back rank**. But more critically, after Bxe7, if black recaptures with the king (Kxe7), then the king goes to e7 — still exposed, but perhaps worse for black? Or maybe we open lines.\n\nBut better idea: **B: c5-e7** attacks the f8 square, and if black doesn\'t respond, next move could be something like Re1-e6+! — rook lift! Because after bishop takes on e7, the e-file becomes open: e5 pawn is white, but if we remove the bishop from c5, nothing blocks — wait, no: the pawn on e5 is still there, blocking the rook on e2 from reaching beyond e5.\n\nUnless we push or capture.\n\nAlternative plan: **Target the e6 king with discovered attack or prepare rook lift**.\n\nBut look at **pawn on f2**: can play f2-g3, preparing bishop to g2, eyeing the a8-h1 diagonal.\n\nBut slower.\n\nWait — **B: c5-e7** — what happens? If black plays Kxe7, then the king is on e7, and now the rook on e2 can play **e2-e6+!**, check! Because e5 is empty? No — e5 has a **white pawn (PWF1)** — still there. So rook cannot go to e6.\n\nUnless we first remove the e5 pawn — but it\'s ours.\n\nSo e5 pawn blocks our own rook.\n\nThat’s a problem.\n\nAlternatively, **can we play e5-e6?** But it\'s a pawn, and it\'s not in the list of valid moves — because pushing e5 to e6 is not listed, meaning it\'s either pinned or not legal? Wait, why isn\'t P: e5-e6 a valid move?\n\nLooking at the valid moves: only P: a2-a4, a2-a3, b2-b4, etc. — no e5-e6. That means the e5 pawn cannot move — probably because it\'s pinned? Or because it\'s not White\'s turn to move that? No — it\'s our turn.\n\nWait — the e5 pawn is on e5, and its possible moves would be e5-e6 or e5xf6. But f6 is empty, so no capture. e6 has the black king — cannot capture king, and also, pawns don\'t capture straight forward.\n\nSo e5 pawn can only move to e6 if empty — but e6 has the black king — occupied. So **e5 pawn cannot move at all** — it\'s blocked by the black king on e6. So it\'s frozen. That explains why it\'s not in the valid moves list.\n\nSo the e5 pawn is blocking both the rook on e2 and preventing advancement.\n\nTherefore, rook on e2 can only move to e1, e3, e4 — not beyond.\n\nSo rook lift is blocked.\n\nBut here\'s an idea: **Bishop on c5 captures on e3** — B: c5-e3.\n\nWhy e3? Because it opens the possibility of discovered attack if we later move the e2 rook.\n\nCurrently, the rook on e2 and bishop on c5 are aligned — but not in a way that allows discovery on the king.\n\nBut if we play B: c5-e3, then the bishop is on e3, and rook on e2 can slide up — but still blocked by e5 pawn.\n\nNo help.\n\nAnother idea: **Target weak back rank**.\n\nBlack has only rook on a8 and knight on a6 — other pieces undeveloped. King on e6 is out in the open.\n\nBut how to exploit?\n\nLook at **b2-b4** or **a2-a4** — flank pawns, but slow.\n\nWait — **rook on b1**: can move to a1, c1, d1, e1, f1, g1.\n\nIf I play **R: b1-e1**, then I double rooks on e-file? But e2 has rook, e1 would have another — but they are on different files? No — b1 is on b-file. Rb1 to e1 is moving across.\n\nYes — rook from b1 to e1 is allowed — it\'s horizontal.\n\nAfter R: b1-e1, then I have **two rooks on the e-file**: one on e1, one on e2 — but still blocked by the e5 pawn.\n\nBut if the e5 pawn ever moves (though it can\'t now), or if it\'s captured, then the file opens.\n\nBut currently, no immediate threat.\n\nBack to bishop: **B: c5-f8** is a valid move.\n\nf8 is empty. If I play B: c5-f8, then I occupy f8 — a key square behind black’s lines. From f8, the bishop eyes e7, d6, and especially **g7**, where black has a bishop (BBF1).\n\nBut more importantly, **after Bxf8, if black recaptures with the king (Kxf8), then the king goes to f8**, which might seem safe, but then I can play **R: e2-e6+!**, delivering check!\n\nBecause: e2 to e6 — e3, e4, e5 — e5 has white pawn, still there — so rook cannot pass.\n\nAgain, blocked by our own pawn.\n\nSame issue.\n\nSo **no rook lift is possible until the e5 pawn is gone or moved**.\n\nBut it can\'t move — blocked by king.\n\nUnless the king moves away from e6.\n\nSo perhaps I need to **threaten something so strong that black must move the king**, exposing it further or allowing entry.\n\nBest chance: **discovered attack** or **check**.\n\nIs there a