ut not h1? That can’t be.\n\nWait — h1 has the king — KWF1 — so rook cannot go to h1 — occupied.\n\nSo rook on b1 can go to a1, c1, d1, e1, f1, g1 — yes, g1 is last before h1.\n\nSo R: b1-g1 places rook on g1, adjacent to king.\n\nBut not useful immediately.\n\nAnother idea: **prepare Q-side attack**.\n\nBut no queen mentioned — probably on d1, unmoved, but not shown because no piece listed on d1.\n\nIn initial setup, queen should be on d1 for white.\n\nBut in the board, d1 is empty — no piece listed.\n\nSimilarly, f1, g1, etc.\n\nBut rook is on b1, king on h1 — so queen must be on d1, but not listed — meaning it’s been captured? Or the representation is incomplete?\n\nWait — the board shows:\n- a8: Rook\n- b8: empty\n- ...\n- d1: not listed — so empty?\nBut in standard chess, white queen starts on d1.\n\nThis suggests that the queen has been captured or moved.\n\nBut in the valid moves, no queen moves are listed — only pawns, rooks, bishop, king.\n\nSo likely, I have **lost my queen**.\n\nSimilarly, black may have lost theirs.\n\nSo material is reduced.\n\nGiven that, perhaps simplification is good.\n\nBut black king is exposed — I should attack.\n\nLet’s reconsider **B: c5-e7**.\n\nAfter Bxe7, if black plays Kxe7, then king is on e7.\n\nThen I can play **R: e2-e6+!** — but again, e5 has my own pawn — blocks.\n\nUnless — can I **capture the e5 pawn myself**? No, it’s mine.\n\nOr push it — but it’s blocked.\n\nOnly way is if black moves the king from e6, freeing e5 pawn to move — but it can’t move because it’s blocked, and when unblocked, it could advance.\n\nBut not helpful.\n\nWait — what if I **sacrifice the bishop**?\n\nFor example, **B: c5-e7**, and if Kxe7, then **R: e2-e6+**, but still blocked.\n\nNo.\n\nAnother idea: **B: c5-b4**\n\nb4 is empty. This develops the bishop to a strong central square, controlling e1-a5 diagonal.\n\nFrom b4, it attacks a5, c5, d6, e7, f8 — and importantly, **a3 is not relevant**.\n\nBut also, it pins the e5 pawn? No.\n\nMore impo