ck’s. Similarly, g8 has RBF1 — Black rook. But f8 is occupied by **my own bishop** (BWF1). That means I have a bishop trapped on f8, potentially under threat from the black rook on g6 if they move to f6 or g8.\n\nBut looking at the board:\n\n- f8: BWF1 → White Bishop\n- g6: RBF1 → Black Rook\n- e6: KBF1 → Black King\n\nSo my bishop on f8 is currently safe—it can be developed via Bf8-e7, d6, c5, etc.\n\nBut more importantly: **Black's king is on e6**, which is extremely exposed. It's on an open file, and I have multiple pieces that could target this square. My rook on e2 is on the same file! e2-e5 or even e2-e6+ would be strong ideas.\n\nAlso, note: if I play R: e2-e6+, that would be check—attacking the black king directly. Is it legal?\n\nYes: the rook moves vertically any number of squares. From e2 to e6: are there any blocking pieces?\n\nCheck file e:\n\n- e1: empty\n- e2: RWF1 (my rook)\n- e3: empty\n- e4: empty\n- e5: BBF1 (Black bishop)\n- e6: KBF1 (Black king)\n\nSo e5 is occupied by a black bishop. Therefore, **I cannot play e2-e6** because the bishop blocks the path.\n\nBut I can consider **capturing the bishop first**: R: e2-e5.\n\nThat would trade rook for bishop, but also place me one step closer to attacking the king. After Rxe5, the black king is still on e6, and now I control e5.\n\nBut better yet: what about **developing the bishop on f8**? It's mine, and it's stuck in the back rank. Moving it out (e.g., Bf8-c5) could open diagonals toward the black king.\n\nLooking at possible bishop moves: B: f8-c5 is valid. From f8 to c5: diagonal? f8-e7-d6-c5: yes, that's a diagonal. Is anything blocking?\n\nf8 → e7 → d6 → c5\n\n- e7: empty\n- d6: empty\n- c5: empty\n\nSo yes, Bf8-c5 is possible and threatens the black king along the long diagonal.\n\nMoreover, after Bc5, the bishop eyes the weak f2-b6 diagonal and could support future attacks. Also, it increases pressure on e6.\n\nAlternatively, Bf8-g7 develops more safely, but less aggressively.\n\nAnother idea: