, d7 — wait: knight on f6 controls e4 and d5, but **not e5**. e5 is a dark square; knight on f6 (a light-square jumper) does not attack e5.\n\nCheck knight moves: from f6, 2 in one direction + 1 perpendicular:\n- f6 → g8, h7, h5, g4, e4, d5, d7, e8 — indeed, **e5 is not attacked by knight**.\n\nBlack bishop on e5 — is it defended?  \n- Backward along diagonal: from e5 to d4, c3… no black piece.  \n- No other black piece appears to defend it.\n\nTherefore, **R: e2-e5** wins a piece (bishop) for free.\n\nThat’s a major material gain — going from likely losing to winning an exchange.\n\nOther moves like **B: g5-f4** or **g3-g4** don’t offer such concrete gains.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended black bishop on e5 with the rook (R: e2-e5) wins material immediately and increases my winning chances significantly. It’s safe (no immediate recapture), improves piece activity, and disrupts Black’s coordination.\n\nAnswer: R: e2-e5"}
