al threat — Black can play ...d5 or ...Kc5 or even ...Rxe7 if not careful. But currently, the black king on b6 is dangerously exposed. My bishop on e7 is attacking along the long diagonal — from e7 to a3, which passes near b6.\n\nLet’s see: from e7 to b6 is diagonal (e7–d6–c5–b4–a3)? No — e7 to b6 is three left, one down — not diagonal. Wait: e7 to b6 is -3 files, -1 rank — not a diagonal. So bishop on e7 does **not** attack b6.\n\nBut what diagonals does it control? From e7:  \n- a3 (e7→d6→c5→b4→a3) — yes, same color square (dark).  \n- h4 (e7→f6→g5→h4) — yes.  \n- Also f8, d8, etc.\n\nSo the bishop on e7 controls a3, b4, c5, d6, f6, g5, h4 — all dark squares.\n\nBlack king on b6 is on a light square — so not directly attacked by bishop. But b6 is very weak — surrounded by light and dark squares.\n\nNow, possible threats: Black may try to advance the a-pawn or bring the king toward safety. But right now, Black has no immediate threat against my bishop on e7 — unless they play ...Kc5 or ...Kc6, but that would be risky.\n\nMy priorities:  \n1. Exploit the exposed black king.  \n2. Avoid losing material unnecessarily.  \n3. Develop or improve piece activity.\n\nLooking at valid moves:\n\nPawn moves:  \n- a2-a4 or a2-a3  \n- b2-b4 or b2-b3  \n- c4-c5\n\nRook moves: b1 to any file on rank 1  \nBishop moves: e7 to various squares — including d6, c5, b4, a3 (attacking closer to black king), or retreating  \nKing moves: h1-g1 (kingside shuffle), h1-g2, h1-h2\n\nKey idea: Can I attack the black king?\n\nFrom current position, no direct check. But let’s see if I can prepare something.\n\nIf I play **Bishop to c5** (B: e7-c5), that places the bishop on c5, which is only two squares away from b6 — and on a diagonal pointing at b6. From c5, the bishop eyes b6 — and if Black ever plays ...Ka5 or ...Kc6, we might exploit.\n\nBut more importantly: **if I play Bc5, and then later support with Qa3 or something — but I don’t have a queen out**.\n\nAlternatively, **Bb4** (B: e7-b4) pu