\n1 |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, R: b1-a1, R: b1-c1, R: b1-d1, R: b1-e1, R: b1-f1, R: b1-g1, R: b1-h1, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c4-c5, B: e7-d6, B: e7-c5, B: e7-b4, B: e7-a3, B: e7-f6, B: e7-g5, B: e7-h4, B: e7-d8, B: e7-f8, K: h2-g1, K: h2-g2, K: h2-h1, K: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. Black has a significant material advantage with two rooks (RBF1 on h5 and likely another on a8 or off-board), a king (KBF1 on b6), and a bishop (BWF1 on e7 — wait, this is actually my own bishop, misread initially). Correction: BWF1 is *my* White-squared bishop on e7, which is unusual since bishops usually don’t start there — but per the state, it's present. Black has pawns on a7 and h4, a king on b6, and a rook on h5. My position: king on h2 (KWF1), rook on b1 (RWF1), pawns on a2, b2, c4; bishop on e7.\n\nWait — correction: **BWF1** means **White Bishop**, so yes, I have a bishop on e7? But that’s deep in enemy territory. That seems like a tactical anomaly. Let's re-analyze carefully.\n\nActually, looking again:\n\n- **e7**: BWF1 → White Bishop on e7 — very advanced, possibly en prise?\n- But no black pieces are immediately attacking it.\n- Black king is on b6, far away.\n- Black pawn on h4, rook on h5, pawn on a7.\n\nMy king is on h2, somewhat exposed but not under immediate threat.\n\nBut crucially: **Black king is on b6**, which is an unusually exposed position, especially for a late-stage game. It has limited mobility.\n\nMy pieces:\n- Pawns: a2 (PWF0), b2 (PWF0), c4 (PWF1)\n- Bishop: e7 (BWF1) — already moved, not en passant eligible\n- Rook: b1 (RWF1)\n- King: h2 (KWF1)\n\nI can push pawns or develop pieces.\n\nValid moves include:\n- Pawn advances: a2-a3/a4, b2-b3/b4, c4-c5\n- Rook moves: b1 to any square on rank 1\n- Bishop moves from e7 to various diagonals (d6, c5, b4, a3, f6, g5, h4, d8, f8)\n- King moves: h2-g1, g2, h1, h3\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **Bishop on e7**: Very active, but possibly vulnerable. Moving it to **b4** attacks the black king directly — check! B: e7-b4+ would be a discovered attack or direct check. Is that possible?\n\nCheck if b4 is reachable: e7 to b4 is diagonal (same color?), e7 is light square, b4 is dark — no! Wait: e7 is light (e=file 5, odd; 7=odd → light), b4: b=2 even, 4 even → light. Yes, same color. So diagonal exists. e7→b4 is along the a8-h1 diagonal? No: e7 to b4 is 3 left, 3 down — yes, that’s a valid diagonal.\n\nSo **B: e7-b4+** gives check to the black king on b6. That’s a strong move!\n\nAfter **B: e7-b4+**, king must respond:\n- K: b6-a6\n- K: b6-a7\n- K: b6-c7\n- K: b6-c6 (but still attacked by bishop?)\n\nWait: b4 to b6 is vertical, not diagonal. Bishop on b4 attacks along diagonals, not files. So from b4, it attacks a3, c5, d6, e7, f8 and also a5, c3, d2, etc. Does it attack b6?\n\nNo! b4 and b6 are on the same file. Bishop cannot attack vertically. So **B: e7-b4 does NOT attack b6**. Mistake!\n\nDistance: e7 to b6 is 3 left, 1 down — not diagonal. So not a direct attack.\n\nWhat squares from e7 *do* attack b6?\n\nLet’s compute: b6 is b-file, 6th rank.\n\nFrom e7 (e,7), moving to:\n- d6: e→d (-1), 7→6 (-1): yes, diagonal. From d6, does it attack b6? d6 to b6 is -2 on file, same rank — horizontal, so no.\n\nCan the bishop attack b6 from any square?\n\nOnly if it’s on a diagonal intersecting b6.\n\nDiagonals through b6:\n- a7-c5-d4-e3-f2-g1\n- c7-d8\n- a5-c7-d8\n- c5-d4-e3-f2-g1-h0 (invalid)\n- Or backward: b6-a5, b6-c7, b6-d8; and b6-a7, b6-c5, b6-d4, b6-e3, b6-f2, b6-g1, b6-h0\n\nSo only if bishop is on one of those.\n\nFrom e7, can it reach any square that attacks b6?\n\nYes: for example, **c5** is on the b6-a5 diagonal? b6 to c5 is +1,+1 — yes, same diagonal. So bishop on c5 attacks b6.\n\ne7 → c5: e to c is -2, 7 to 5 is -2 → yes, diagonal move. So **B: e7-c5** is possible and attacks b6.\n\nSimilarly, **b4**: e7 to b4 is -3, -3 → yes, but b4 is not on b6 diagonal.\n\nFrom b4, diagonals: a3, c5, d6, e7… and a5, c3, d2… no b6.\n\nSo only **B: e7-c5** attacks b6.\n\nIs c5 occupied? Looking at board: c5 is empty. Yes.\n\nSo **B: e7-c5+** gives check to black king on b6.\n\nThat’s powerful.\n\nOther options:\n- **B: e7-g5**: targets h4 pawn? Or prepares support?\n- **B: e7-d6**: central, but not threatening\n- **Pawn pushes**: a2-a4 or b2-b4 to gain space\n- **Rook activation**: R: b1-a1 or R: b1-c1 — but not much target yet\n- **King moves**: K: h2-g2 — improving king safety slightly\n\nBest candidate: **B: e7-c5+**, giving check and attacking the black king.\n\nAfter B: e7-c5+, Black king must move:\n- K: b6-a6 (out of check)\n- K: b6-a7\n- K: b6-c7\n- K: b6-c6 (but still on diagonal?) — c6 is not on c5-b6 diagonal? c5 to c6 is vertical. Bishop on c5 attacks b6 and d4, etc., but not c6 unless diagonal.\n\nb6 to c5 is diagonal, so reverse: c5 to b6 is same line. So yes, if king goes to c6, it’s not on that diagonal. Only b6 is attacked from c5 along that line.\n\nWait: from c5, bishop attacks:\n- Up-right: d6, e7, f8\n- Down-left: b6, a7\n- Up-left: b4, a3\n- Down-right: d4, e3, f2, g1\n\nSo yes, **b6 is attacked from c5**.\n\nSo if king moves to a6, a7, c7, c6 — all except c6 might be safe? c6: is c6 on the diagonal? c5 to c6 is vertical — no. So c6 is not attacked. Similarly, a6, a7, c7 are not on the diagonal.\n\nBut **a7** is on the diagonal from c5: c5 → b6 → a7. Yes! So a7 is attacked.\n\nSo after B: e7-c5+, black king cannot go to a7 (attacked), cannot stay on b6 (attacked), so only escapes: a6, c6, c7.\n\nAll are possible, but it loses material or weakens.\n\nMoreover, after this, I may follow up with rook lift or other threats.\n\nAlternatively, **R: b1-a1** prepares pressure on a-file, eyeing the black pawn on a7.\n\nOr **P: a2-a4** to prevent ...b5 or prepare b4 later.\n\nBut **B: e7-c5+** is a forcing move — checks the king, limits responses, and increases pressure.\n\nIs the bishop safe on c5? Can it be captured?\n\nCurrently, no black piece attacks c5. Black has:\n- Rook on h5 — doesn't reach c5\n- Pawn on h4 — no\n- King on b6 — too far\n- Pawn on a7 — no\n\nSo no immediate capture. Bishop is safe.\n\nTherefore, **B: e7-c5** is strong.\n\nNow, is it in the valid move list?\n\nLooking: Valid moves include: **B: e7-c5**\n\nYes! It's listed.\n\nOther good moves:\n- **B: e7-b4**: threatens nothing directly, but pins? No pin possible.\n- **R: b1-h1**: long-range, but no target\n- **P: b2-b4**: gains space, but slow\n\nReason: **B: e7-c5+** delivers check, forces the black king to a worse position, and activates a powerful piece with tempo. It maximizes pressure and minimizes Black's counterplay. It's the most forcing and advantageous move.\n\nAnswer: B: e7-c5"}
