s — f2 and d2 are on the same rank! But bishop moves diagonally, not horizontally.\n\nWait — critical mistake: bishop on c5 to f2: c5 is file c, rank 5; f2 is file f, rank 2. Δfile = +3, Δrank = -3 → yes, that is a diagonal move — so it\'s legal. Now, what does this bishop attack after f2? It sits on the a6-f1 diagonal. Does it attack anything? The black rook on d2 is on rank 2, file d. From f2, the bishop would control the diagonal f2-c5-b4-a3 — and also f2-g3-h4. Not d2 — d2 is two files left, same rank — that\'s horizontal, not diagonal. So no direct capture.\n\nBut more importantly — is there a threat? The black pawn on g3 (PBF1) is on g3 — which is adjacent to h2, where my king is. Currently, g3 is occupied by a black pawn. Can it capture my king? No — pawns capture diagonally forward. Black pawn on g3 captures on f2 or h2 — yes! h2 is exactly where my white king is. And since it\'s black\'s turn next? No — wait, it\'s *my* turn as White. After I move, Black will respond.\n\nBut if I do nothing, and I leave my king on h2, and black has a pawn on g3, then if I don\'t block or move, can that pawn capture my king? Only if it\'s legally able to move to h2. But pawns capture diagonally forward — for black, forward is downward. So black pawn on g3 moves down to g2, g1, etc., and captures on f2 or h2 — but only if those are occupied by enemy pieces. So yes, the black pawn on g3 attacks h2 — which is where my king is!\n\nSo my king is currently under attack by the black pawn on g3? Is that true?\n\nBlack pawn on g3: color Black, so it moves down (ranks decreasing). It captures diagonally down — so from g3, it can capture on f2 or h2. My white king is on h2 — so yes, **my king is in check** from the black pawn on g3.\n\nBut that means I must get out of check immediately.\n\nTherefore, the only legal moves are those that remove the king from h2 or block the pawn\'s attack — but pawns cannot be blocked — checks by pawns must be resolved by capturing the pawn or moving the