and gaining space.\n\nEven better: **R: f1-f4** – centralizes the rook, avoids passive placement, eyes the fourth rank, supports potential threats against g3 or h3.\n\nBut no immediate target.\n\nWait — look at **P: a2-a4** – not urgent.\n\nBest idea: **Activate the rook to a more useful square**.\n\nConsider **R: f1-a1** – clears the back rank, connects with potential a2 pawn, but not urgent.\n\nOr **R: f1-f2** – supports the g2 square, guards against g3-g2 ideas, but g3 is occupied by Black’s pawn.\n\nBlack’s pawn on g3 is on g3 — that’s behind my lines. It’s actually *my* g2 square that matters.\n\nWait — g3 is Black’s pawn. So it’s on *my* side. That pawn could promote if unchecked.\n\ng3 is on rank 3 — so it\'s one step from g2, then g1. If it reaches g1, it promotes.\n\nSo **the pawn on g3 is extremely dangerous**.\n\nIt\'s on g3, Black\'s turn next — but it\'s currently White\'s turn.\n\nAfter my move, Black will be able to play g3-g2, then on next move g2-g1=Q, promoting to queen.\n\nUnless I stop it.\n\nCan I stop it?\n\nCurrent position: Black pawn on g3 (PBF1). My king on h2. My rook on f1. No other pieces near.\n\nAfter my move, Black plays g3-g2.\n\nThen it\'s on g2, attacking h1 and ready to promote.\n\nOn my next turn, I could capture it if it\'s unprotected — but if it promotes, I lose unless I can recapture.\n\nBut if it promotes to queen, I lose material unless I checkmate first.\n\nSo I must prevent g3-g2 from being unstoppable.\n\nIs there a way to capture the pawn now?\n\nCan I play **K: h2-g3**? Yes — that’s in the move list: **K: h2-g3**\n\nKing takes pawn on g3!\n\nYes! **K: h2-g3** captures the black pawn on g3.\n\nThat removes the passed pawn, eliminates the promotion threat, and centralizes my king slightly.\n\nAfter Kxg3, Black has no immediate promotion, and I’m safe.\n\nIs it safe for me? After Kxg3, my king is on g3. Is it attacked? Black has a bishop on a7 — can it attack g3?\n\nBishop on a7: color of squares — a7 is black square. Diagonals: a7-h7 is light diagonal? Wait:\n\na7 = dark square (a=file 1 odd, 7=odd → dark)\n\ng3 = g=7th file, odd, 3=odd → dark square. So same color.\n\nNow, is there a diagonal from a7 to g3?\n\nFrom a7 to g3: difference of +6 files, -4 ranks → slope = 4/6 = 2/3 — not diagonal. Diagonal requires equal file/rank change.\n\na7 to g3: Δfile = +6, Δrank = -4 → not equal → not on same diagonal.\n\nLongest diagonal from a7 is to h7 (horizontal), or to b6, c5, d4, e3, f2, g1.\n\ng1 is on the diagonal — yes: a7-b6-c5-d4-e3-f2-g1.\n\nSo g3 is *not* on that diagonal. g3 is two squares above g1.\n\nSo bishop on a7 does **not** attack g3.\n\nTherefore, **K: h2-g3** is safe — no capture possible by Black’s bishop.\n\nAfter Kxg3, Black must respond — likely moving king or bishop.\n\nI eliminate a dangerous passed pawn, improve king activity, and simplify the endgame.\n\nThis is clearly the best move.\n\nOther moves allow g3-g2, leading to likely promotion and loss.\n\nTherefore:\n\nAnswer: K: h2-g3'}
