, f1-g1, f1-h1 – Rook lifts could be useful later, but currently lack targets.\n- K: g3-f3, g3-h3, g3-f4 – King movement to centralize or shelter. f4 could be strong, supporting e5 or g5 pushes. f2 supports rook activation.\n- B: a7-x – All black bishop moves: irrelevant since I control the move.\n\nMost promising:\n- P: c4-c5 – Strong central push, limits Black’s king mobility, gains space, and accelerates my development.\n- K: g3-f4 – Centralizes the king, potentially preparing Ke5 or Kf5, very active.\n- R: f1-f4 – Supports Kg3-f4, doubles on f-file, increases pressure.\n\nHowever, **P: c4-c5** stands out because it seizes space immediately, restricts the black king, and doesn’t lose tempo. It also avoids passivity. Black may struggle to respond effectively, especially with their pieces undeveloped.\n\nReason: c4-c5 is a proactive, central pawn break that limits Black’s counterplay and improves my positional dominance. It prepares potential piece play with Bc5 or Qa4+ ideas later, even though I don’t have a queen yet — but it sets the stage.\n\nAnswer: P: c4-c5'}
