 or b2-b4** – aim to promote?\n   - Distant end of board; takes many moves to promote.\n   - But with opposite-colored king, might be useful.\n   - However, Black king is on c8 — far from queenside.\n   - So advancing a- or b-pawn could create passed pawns.\n\n4. **Rook moves: R: f1-f7? But not listed. Only listed rook moves are to ranks 1–8 files a–h. Looking: R: f1-f8 is valid? Yes — along file.\n   - Rf1-f8 could threaten back-rank ideas.\n   - But Black has no pieces on 8th rank except king on c8 — not near f8.\n   - Rf1-f6, f5, etc., less useful.\n\n   Best rook move might be **Rf1-c1**, centralizing, eyeing the c-file against Black king.\n\n5. **King move: g3-g4** – activates king, centralizes.\n   - But not urgent.\n   - King is safe enough.\n\n6. **Pawn c6-c7!** – very strong idea.\n   - P: c6-c7 advances the pawn to the 7th rank.\n   - Next move, c7-c8=Q will promote unless stopped.\n   - Black must respond immediately.\n   - Black king is on c8 — if c7 advances, then c8 is attacked, so king must move.\n   - After c6-c7, Black king is in check? No — c7 does not attack c8.\n   - c7 attacks d8 and b8, but not c8.\n   - So Kc8 is not in check.\n   - But next move, c8=Q is coming, and Black must prevent it.\n   - Only way: play ...Kxc7? But then my bishop on a7 is still there — after ...Kxc7, Ba7-b6+ forks king and possibly wins something?\n     - Let's see:\n       - 1. c7, Kxc7\n       - 2. Ba7-b6+ → Kb8 (only)\n       - 3. Rf1-c1, attacking pinned king on b8? Not pinned.\n       - But Rc1-c8+ would win the game? Not yet.\n     - Alternatively, after 1...Kd7, then 2. c8=Q, promotion.\n\n   So **P: c6-c7** is a powerful move, forcing either:\n   - Black allows c8=Q next move, or\n   - Black captures on c7, allowing Ba7-b6+ with discovery pressure.\n\n   This gains material or promotes.\n\nReason: Promoting the c-pawn is the fastest path to decisive advantage. It creates an immediate threat of queening, forcing Black into a losing position. Even if captured, the follow-up with the bishop and rook can dominate.\n\nOther moves like B: a7-b6 or B: a7-c5 are slow in comparison.\n\nBest move: **P: c6-c7**\n\nAnswer: P: c6-c7"}
