\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |RBF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, R: f8-e8, R: f8-d8, R: f8-c8, R: f8-b8, R: f8-a8, R: f8-f7, R: f8-f6, R: f8-f5, R: f8-f4, R: f8-f3, R: f8-f2, R: f8-f1, R: f8-g8, R: f8-h8, K: g3-f2, K: g3-f3, K: g3-f4, K: g3-g2, K: g3-g4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on g3 (KWF1), a white rook is on f8 (RWF1), and two pawns on a2 and b2 (both PWF0). The only black piece visible is a rook on h1 (RBF1) and a king on b7 (KBF1). Black’s king is far from the action, and their rook is passive. My rook on f8 is well-placed centrally and can exert control over the entire 8th rank and file. My king is somewhat exposed but not in immediate danger. The pawns on a2 and b2 are ready to advance.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Advancing pawns (a2-a4, a2-a3, b2-b4, b2-b3): These moves aim to push pawns forward, potentially promoting them. However, with no black pieces nearby and the black king distant, immediate pawn promotion is unlikely soon. Still, gaining space and eventually queening a pawn could be decisive in an endgame.\n- Rook moves (e.g., f8-f1, f8-b8, etc.): Moving the rook to f1 would attack the black rook on h1 via the back rank. However, the black rook is already on h1 — if it\'s truly on h1 (RBF1), then f8-f1 would directly threaten it. But wait: RBF1 is on h1? According to the board layout, the piece on h1 is labeled RBF1, but in the diagram it\'s shown in the last cell of rank 1 — which is h1. So yes,