 a queen, since material is otherwise equal or worse for me.\n- Black may try to activate their king or rook, but with my pawns advancing, they may not have time.\n- Moving the rook from h8 (e.g., R: h8-h7) trades rooks, which is bad — I should avoid unnecessary trades when I can push a pawn.\n- King moves like g3-f3 or g3-f4 don’t help much — the king is not needed here yet.\n- Best plan: Start advancing a pawn to promote. The a-pawn and b-pawn are both candidates. Since they’re symmetric, pushing either two squares is best to start the run.\n\na2-a4 and b2-b4 are both strong. Let’s compare:\n- a2-a4: opens the a-file, less likely to be blocked later.\n- b2-b4: same idea.\n\nBut no immediate difference. Either is fine. However, pushing a2-a4 slightly increases the chance of creating a passed pawn earlier, especially if b-pawn follows.\n\nAlso, moving a2-a4 first might allow b2-b4 later without blocking the a-file for a potential rook lift — though rook activity is low priority now.\n\nReason: The key to winning this endgame is promoting a pawn before Black can coordinate their pieces. Since Black’s king is far away on b7, they will take many moves to approach. I must act quickly. Starting with a two-square pawn move maximizes tempo. Between a2-a4 and b2-b4, both are good. I’ll pick a2-a4 to begin advancing the a-pawn, keeping options open.\n\nAnswer: P: a2-a4"}
