\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a5, R: a8-a7, R: a8-a6, R: a8-a5, R: a8-b8, R: a8-c8, R: a8-d8, R: a8-e8, R: a8-f8, R: a8-g8, R: a8-h8, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, K: g3-f3, K: g3-f4, K: g3-g4, K: g3-h3, K: g3-h4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly simplified with very few pieces remaining. My only pieces are a rook on a8 (RWF1), a pawn on a4 (PWF1), a pawn on b2 (PWF0), and my king on g3 (KWF1). Black has only a rook on d2 (RBF1) and a king on b7 (KBF1). The center and most of the board are empty. My king is in the kingside, while Black’s king is on the queenside. There is a significant material imbalance, but both sides have drawing chances if not careful.\n\nKey observations:\n- Both my pawns are potentially mobile. The b2-pawn can advance immediately.\n- The a4-pawn is already on the 4th rank and could push to a5.\n- The rook on a8 is undeveloped and can be activated along the 8th or a-file.\n- My king is somewhat exposed but not under immediate threat.\n- Black’s king is far away, which may allow me to promote a pawn if advanced aggressively.\n- The b2 pawn is on its starting square and can move two squares (b2-b4) or one (b2-b3).\n- Promoting either pawn would likely win the game, so advancing them efficiently is key.\n- The b-pawn is closer to queening (6 moves from promotion) than the a-pawn (5 moves: a5-a6-a7-a8), but the a-pawn is already further advanced.\n\nEvaluation of promising moves:\n1. P: b2-b4 – Activates the b-pawn, gains space, opens possibility for rapid advancement. However, it skips b3, which might be useful for blocking or coordination. Also risks being blocked or captured if Black's king comes near.\n2. P: b2-b3 – Safer advance, supports potential a4 support or king walk, keeps options open. But slower.\n3. P: a4-a5 – Pushes the a-pawn, maintaining momentum toward promotion. This pawn is only 3 more pushes from queening (a5, a6, a7, a8=Q). It also opens the a-file for the rook on a8.\n4. R: a8-a7 – Moves rook toward the 7th rank, possibly preparing activity. But currently passive and slow.\n5. K: g3-f3 / g3-f4 / g3-g4 / etc. – King moves toward center, but with no enemy pieces nearby, this may be premature. King should eventually centralize or assist in pawn promotion, but not urgent.\n\nBest plan: Accelerate a passed pawn. The a-pawn is further advanced and pushing it to a5 prepares a6 and then a7, threatening queening. Unlike the b-pawn, the a-pawn doesn’t need to worry about c-file complications and is on an edge where the opposing king may struggle to stop it. Moreover, pushing a4-a5 now maintains tempo and initiative.\n\nAdditionally, after a4-a5, next move can be a5-a6, and the rook can double on the a-file to support promotion. Black’s king is too far (b7) to intervene quickly.\n\nReason: Pushing the a-pawn increases my most immediate winning chances by advancing a passed pawn toward promotion. It gains space and prepares to activate the rook. The move a4-a5 is more urgent than b2-b4 because the a-pawn is closer to queening and has a clearer path.\n\nAnswer: P: a4-a5"}
