\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a7-a8, R: a7-b7, R: a7-c7, R: a7-d7, R: a7-e7, R: a7-f7, R: a7-g7, R: a7-h7, P: b4-b5, K: g3-f2, K: g3-f3, K: g3-f4, K: g3-g2, K: g3-g4, K: g3-h2, K: g3-h3, K: g3-h4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king (KWF1) is on g3, exposed but not in immediate check. The only active pieces I have are a rook on a7 (RWF1), a pawn on b4 (PWF1), and the king. Black has a king on b8 (KBF1) and rook on c8 (RBF1), both undeveloped, and a rook on a8. Black’s pieces appear passive, but my position is precarious due to limited material and development.\n\nMy rook on a7 is on the 7th rank and can immediately promote if it reaches a8. Currently, a8 is occupied by Black’s own rook, which is immobile for now. However, capturing on a8 would be a major advantage — promoting a rook to a queen would drastically shift the balance.\n\nThe pawn on b4 can advance to b5, but that doesn’t create immediate threats. The king has several moves, but they are mostly shuffling around with no clear progress.\n\nEvaluation:\n- R: a7-a8: Captures Black’s rook and promotes to queen (implied promotion). This is extremely strong, likely decisive. After this move, I will have a queen on a8, which controls the entire a-file and 8th rank, putting immense pressure on Black’s king and remaining rook.\n- P: b4-b5: A waiting move with no immediate threat. Wastes tempo while Black can activate their pieces.\n- King moves (e.g., K: g3-f3, etc.): Do nothing meaningful. The king is safe enough for now; moving it gains no advantage.\n\nReason: Capturing on a8 with the rook is the only move that creates a decisive material gain. It removes Black’s rook and promotes to a queen (standard rules allow rook promotion, though typically players promote pawns — but here the notation suggests the rook itself can move to a8, implying capture and possible underpromotion or misnotation. Wait — correction: rooks don’t "promote" by capturing. Only pawns promote upon reaching the last rank.\n\nWait — critical realization: **Only pawns promote**. The rook on a7 moving to a8 is just a rook move, not a promotion. But a7 to a8 captures Black’s rook on a8? But a8 already has a piece: Black\'s rook (RBF1). So R: a7-a8 implies capturing that rook.\n\nYes — R: a7-a8 means White rook captures on a8, removing Black’s rook. That is a free rook for me — winning a full rook for nothing. Black cannot recapture. This is a massive material gain.\n\nEven without promotion, trading my rook for Black’s rook when I’m otherwise down material is excellent — but wait, is it even a trade? No: Black’s rook is on a8, mine on a7. If I play R: a7-a8, I capture their rook. They have no piece defending a8. So I win a rook for free.\n\nCurrently, both sides have one rook each, but Black has an extra rook on c8. Wait — looking back:\n\nBlack has:\n- KBF1 on b8\n- RBF1 on c8\n- And another rook on a8? Wait — the board shows:\n\na8: ??? Wait, the board says:\na8: (empty?) No — let\'s re-read:\n\nRow 8: a: empty? No:\n\n"8 |    |KBF1|RBF1|    |    |    |    |    | 8"\n\nSo:\n- a8: empty\n- b8: KBF1 (Black King)\n- c8: RBF1 (Black Rook)\n\nThen row 7: a7: RWF1 (White Rook)\n\nSo a8 is empty.\n\nTherefore, R: a7-a8 is simply moving the rook to a8 — no capture, no promotion. But why is that a valid move? Because it\'s legal.\n\nBut more importantly: a8 is empty. So moving rook to a8 puts pressure along the 8th rank, directly threatening the Black king on b8 and the rook on c8.\n\nNow, after R: a7-a8, the rook attacks b8 and c8. Black must respond — if they don\'t, next move I can play R: a8-c8, capturing the rook.\n\nBut can I capture the king? No — but I can check the king.\n\nAfter R: a8-a7? No — from a8, rook attacks entire rank and file.\n\nFrom a8, rook attacks b8 (king), so R: a7-a8 gives check to the Black king on b8.\n\nMoreover, the Black king is on b8, attacked by rook on a8 — yes, because same rank.\n\nSo R: a7-a8 checks the Black king.\n\nAdditionally, it threatens to take the rook on c8 next move.\n\nBlack cannot easily escape — king can go to a7 or c7, but a7 is attacked by rook via a-file.\n\nLet’s see:\n\nAfter R: a7-a8+, Black king on b8 is in check.\n\nLegal escapes:\n- b8-a7: but a7 is vacated, but now attacked by rook on a8 → illegal unless captured, but no piece on a7.\nWait — a7 is empty after rook moves to a8.\n\nSo king to a7: is that attacked? Yes — rook on a8 controls a7. So Ka7 is into check — illegal.\n\nb8-c7: c7 is empty, not attacked yet → legal.\n\nb8-b7: b7 is empty? From board: b7 is empty — yes. Is it attacked? Not yet — rook on a8 doesn’t attack b7 (not same file or rank). So Kb7 is legal.\n\nSo Black can escape to c7 or b7.\n\nBut after, say, ...Kc7, then Ra8xc8 wins the rook.\n\nSimilarly, if ...Kb7, then Ra8xc8 also wins rook.\n\nSo in either case, next move I can capture the rook on c8.\n\nThus, R: a7-a8+ forces the king to move, then I win the rook.\n\nThis is winning.\n\nAlternative: do nothing? No — this is the most forcing and strongest move.\n\nPawn move b4-b5 does nothing.\n\nKing moves lose time.\n\nTherefore, R: a7-a8 is best.\n\nBut is it check? Yes — and it’s a powerful one.\n\nNo downside.\n\nAnswer: R: a7-a8'}
