rns: With only a king left, Black is extremely vulnerable.\n- Queen on a8 moving to b7 would attack c6, d5, etc., but more importantly, **Q: a8-a7** attacks b7, c7 — directly threatening the black king on c7.\n  - After Q: a8-a7, Black king is in check and must move.\n  - From c7, possible escapes: b8, b6, c6, d6, d8 — but many of these can be cut off.\n- Alternatively, **R: d8-d7** also puts pressure on d7, attacking the same rank as the black king (rank 7), but not directly checking.\n  - However, Rd7 does not give check — Kb6 or Kc6 would escape easily.\n- Best plan: Use queen to confine the black king, preferably cutting off retreat, then mate with support of king or rook.\n\nBut wait — **Q: a8-b7** gives check! Because b7 is adjacent to c7. Queen moves from a8 to b7 checks the black king directly.\n\nFrom b7, queen controls b-file and 7th rank. After Qb7+, Black king must move:\n- To b8, c8, c6, b6, or d8? Let's see:\n  - b8: covered by queen if it goes to b7\n  - c8: possible\n  - d8: possible\n  - b6: possible\n  - c6: possible\n\nBut after Qb7+, if Black plays Kb8, then Qa7# (but we don’t have that move now — we’re choosing one move).\n\nWait — better: **Q: a8-a7** also gives check! a7 is on the same file as a8, and a7 is adjacent to b7 — but not directly attacking c7.\n\na7 is two files away from c7 — so **Qa7 does NOT attack c7**. Only if queen were on b7, c8, c6, b8, b6 — i.e., adjacent squares.\n\nSo:\n- Distance from a8 to c7: two files and one rank apart — not adjacent. So queen on a8 does not attack c7.\n- But queen on b7 (one square diagonally up-right) **does** attack c7 — yes! b7 is adjacent to c7.\n\nTherefore:\n- **Q: a8-b7** gives check to the black king on c7.\n\nAfter Qb7+, Black king must move:\n- To b8, c8, d8, d7, d6, c6, b6 — but let’s see what’s safe.\n\nBut crucially, after Qb7+, the escape squares:\n- b8: attacked by queen on b7 → invalid\n- c8: not attacked by anything else — possible\n- d8: possible\n- d7: adjacent to queen → yes, attacked → invalid\n- c6: attacked by queen → invalid\n- b6: attacked by queen → invalid\n- d6: not directly attacked yet\n\nSo only **c8**, **d8**, and **d6** are potential escapes.\n\nBut c8 and d8 are on back rank — and I have a rook on d8!\n\nWait — **my rook is on d8**, so:\n- d8 is occupied by my rook → black king cannot go to d8.\n- So after Qb7+, black king can only go to c8 or d6.\n\nIf he goes to c8, then I play **Rd8-d7**, cutting off escape and threatening mate on c7 or b8.\n\nEven better: after Qb7+ Kb8?? — but Kb8 is attacked by queen, so illegal.\n\nOnly legal escapes: c8 and d6.\n\nFrom c8, I can play Rc7 or Qb8#?\n\nWait — if king goes to c8, then **Qb7 gives check**, but king on c8 is not in check from b7? b7 and c8 are adjacent diagonally — yes! So queen on b7 attacks c8.\n\nWait — b7 to c8 is diagonal — one square — yes! So **queen on b7 attacks c8**.\n\nSimilarly, it attacks c7 (horizontal), b8 (vertical), d7 (diagonal), etc.\n\nSo:\n- Queen on b7 attacks:\n  - All squares in b-file: b1–b8\n  - All squares in 7th rank: a7–h7\n  - Diagonals: from a8 to h1 diagonal — includes c6, d5, e4, f3, g2, h1 and a6, b7, c8, d9...\n\nSo b7 attacks c8 (diagonally up-right), c7 (right), b8 (up), d7 (down-right), etc.\n\nThus, after **Q: a8-b7+**, black king on c7 is attacked by queen on b7.\n\nPossible escape squares:\n- b8: attacked by queen (same file)\n- c8: attacked by queen (diagonal)\n- d8: occupied by white rook\n- d7: attacked by queen (same rank)\n- c6: attacked by queen (same file)\n- b6: attacked by queen (same file)\n- d6: not attacked — only possible escape?\n\nIs d6 attacked? Queen on b7: can it reach d6? b7 → c6 → d5 — no, d6 is not on same rank, file, or diagonal.\n\n- Rank: 7 vs 6 → different\n- File: b vs d → different\n- Diagonal: from b7 to c6 to d5 — d6 is not on that diagonal. The diagonal b7-c6-d5 is downward right. d6 would require b7-c7-d7 or b7-c8-d8 — not helpful.\n\nSo d6 is **not** attacked by queen on b7.\n\nAlso, no other white pieces attack d6 — pawn on b4, king on g3 — too far.\n\nSo after Qb7+, black king can escape to **d6**.\n\nThen game continues — not mate yet.\n\nBut perhaps better: use rook to cut off escape.\n\nBest strategy: use rook to control the 7th rank.\n\nPlay **R: d8-d7** — this attacks the entire 7th rank.\n\nAfter Rd7, black king on c7 is attacked — so it's a check.\n\nNow, where can black king go?\n- b8: not attacked by rook (rook on d7, b8 is not same rank or file) — unless diagonal, but rook doesn't do diagonal.\n  - Rook on d7 attacks d-file and 7th rank.\n  - So squares: d1–d8 and a7–h7.\n- So c7 is on 7th rank → attacked.\n- Escape:\n  - b8: not attacked → possible\n  - c8: not attacked → possible\n  - d8: attacked by rook → invalid\n  - d6: not attacked → possible\n  - c6: not attacked → possible\n  - b6: not attacked → possible\n\nSo still many escapes — not good.\n\nBut if I play **Q: a8-c6**, then queen attacks c6 — which is adjacent to c7 — so **check**.\n\nc6 is one square down from c7 — same file.\n\nQueen on c6 attacks:\n- c-file: c1–c8\n- 6th rank: a6–h6\n- Diagonals: b5, a4, d7, e8, etc.\n\nSo after Qc6+, black king must move:\n- b8: not attacked\n- c8: same file → attacked → invalid\n- d8: not attacked\n- d7: attacked by queen (diagonal c6-d7)\n- d6: same rank → attacked → invalid\n- c5: same file → attacked → invalid\n- b7: not attacked\n- b6: same file → attacked → invalid\n\nSo escapes: b8, d8, b7\n\nStill not forced mate.\n\nBut notice: if I play **Q: a8-e4**, long-range threat, but not helpful.\n\nWait — best idea: **use queen to drive king toward edge, then mate with queen and king**.\n\nBut faster: **Q: a8-h1**? Too far.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: a8-g2**? Still far.\n\nWait — what about **Q: a8-a7**?\n\nQueen to a7 — controls a7, so attacks b7 (diagonally), and entire a-file and 7th rank.\n\nBut c7 is on 7th rank — yes! a7 to c7 is two squares over — same rank? a7 and c7 are both on rank 7.\n\nYes! So **Q: a8-a7** places queen on a7, which is on the 7th rank — same as black king on c7.\n\nSo queen on a7 attacks along the 7th rank — so **attacks c7** — thus, **this is a check**.\n\nAfter Qa7+, black king must move.\n\nWhere?\n- b8: not on 7th rank or a-file — not attacked → possible\n- c8: not on 7th rank — not attacked → possible\n- d8: not attacked → possible\n- d7: on 7th rank → attacked by queen → invalid\n- c6: not on 7th rank — not attacked → possible\n- b6: not attacked → possible\n- d6: not attacked → possible\n\nBut also, queen on a7 attacks b7 (diagonally), c7 (same rank), etc.\n\nBut b7 is attacked — so king cannot go to b7.\n\nBut b8 is safe.\n\nSame issue.\n\nBut now consider: **Q: a8-c8**\n\nQueen to c8 — same file as black king (c7), one square above.\n\nSo queen on c8 attacks:\n- c-file: c1–c8 → includes c7\n- 8th rank: a8–h8\n- Diagonals: b7, a6, d7, e6, etc.\n\nSo c8 attacks c7 (directly below) — so **Qc8+ is check**.\n\nAfter Qc8+, black king on c7 must move:\n- b8: attacked? c8 to b8 — same rank, adjacent — yes, queen attacks b8 (left) → invalid\n- c8: occupied\n- d8: same rank → attacked → invalid\n- d7: diagonal — c8 to d7 — yes, one square down-right → attacked → invalid\n- c6: same file → attacked → invalid\n- b7: diagonal — c8 to b7 — yes, one square down-left → attacked → invalid\n- b6: not attacked (not same file, rank, or diagonal) → possible\n- d6: not attacked → possible\n\nSo only **b6** and **d6** are legal escapes.\n\nAgain, not mate.\n\nBut now, compare all checks:\n\nWe want to minimize escape squares and drive king to edge.\n\nBest option: **Q: a8-b7**\n\nWhy? Because b7 is centralizing the queen and cuts off many squares.\n\nBut earlier we saw king escapes to d6.\n\nBut wait — is there a non-check move that forces mate next?\n\nFor example, **R: d8-d7** — not a check, but threatens check and cuts off 7th rank.\n\nBut black can simply move to b6 or c6 or d6 — not forced.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: a8-f3** — too far.\n\nWait — what about **Q: a8-a6**?\n\nNot a check — c7 not attacked.\n\nNo.\n\nAnother idea: **King move?** K: g3-f3 — but king is far away, not helpful.\n\nBut actually, the fastest way to win is to use queen to force the black king toward the edge, then mate with king support.\n\nBut here’s a key insight: **the black king is already near the edge** — on c7.\n\nThe ideal mating pattern is to trap it on the back rank or side.\n\nBut notice: if I play **Q: a8-e4**, it doesn't help.\n\nWait — let's reconsider **R: d8-d7**\n\nAfter Rd7, it attacks the 7th rank — so black king on c7 is in check.\n\nAs before, escapes: b8, c8, d6, c6, b6.\n\nBut now, if black king goes to b8, I can play Qa8#? No, queen is on a8 — but after Rd7, queen is still on a8.\n\nWait — if I play Rd7, and black king goes to b8, then I play **Qa8-a7#** — because a7 attacks b7, and b7 is adjacent to b8 — so queen on a7 attacks b8 (diagonally) — yes!\n\nAfter Rd7+, if black king moves to b8, then Qa7# — because:\n- Queen on a7 attacks b8 (diagonally)\n- Is b8 attacked by anything else? No, but queen alone can deliver mate if supported.\n\nCan black king on b8 move? From b8, possible squares: a8, a7, b7, c8, c7.\n\nAfter Qa7:\n- a8: empty? Yes — but is it attacked? Queen on a7 attacks a8 (up) — yes!\n- a7: occupied by queen\n- b7: attacked by queen (right)\n- c8: not attacked by queen? a7 to c8 — not same file, rank, or diagonal — no\n- c7: same rank — a7 to c7 — yes, attacked\n\nSo king on b8 has:\n- a8: attacked\n- a7: occupied\n- b7: attacked\n- c8: not attacked\n- c7: attacked\n\nSo only escape is c8.\n\nBut c8 is not attacked by queen — so king can go to c8.\n\nSo Qa7 does **not** attack c8 from a7.\n\na7 to c8: difference of +1 file, +1 rank — yes, diagonal! a7 → b7 → c8 — so one step: a7 to b8 is diagonal, a7 to b7 is horizontal, a7 to a8 is vertical.\n\na7 to c8: two files right, one rank up — not a straight line — not attacked.\n\nSo c8 is safe.\n\nThus, after Rd7+ Kb8, Qa7 does **not** give check — because a7 does not attack b8? Wait — a7 and b8 are diagonally adjacent: a7 → b8 is one square up-right — yes! So queen on a7 attacks b8.\n\nYes — same diagonal: a7, b8, c9...\n\nSo b8 is attacked.\n\nAnd a8 is attacked (same file), b7 is attacked (same rank), c7 is attacked (same rank), c8 is not attacked — but king on b8 cannot move to c8 directly — it's knight move.\n\nFrom b8, king moves to:\n- a8: attacked\n- a7: attacked\n- b7: attacked\n- c8: not attacked\n- c7: attacked\n\nSo c8 is the only legal move.\n\nSo after Qa7, king must go to c8.\n\nThen I can play Qa7-c7#? Or something.\n\nBut not immediate mate.\n\nBack to drawing board.\n\nBest move: **Q: a8-b7**\n\nThis is a check, and it maximizes pressure.\n\nIt attacks multiple escape squares.\n\nAfter Qb7+, king can only go to d6 (as previously established — b8, c8, d7, c6, b6 all attacked; d8 occupied).\n\nd6 is the only legal move.\n\nThen, next move, I can play Qb7-d5, for example, attacking d6 again, driving king to edge.\n\nOr better, start bringing my king into play.\n\nBut for now, **Qb7+** forces the black king to d6, centralizing it, but I can follow up with Qd5+ or Qf3+ to drive it to corner.\n\nAlternatively, is there a faster mate?\n\nWhat if I play **Q: a8-d5**?\n\nNot a check — d5 is far from c7.\n\nNo.\n\nOr **Q: a8-g2** — no.\n\nAnother idea: **R: d8-h8** — to prepare rook on h-file, but not helpful.\n\nNo.\n\nBest immediate forcing move is a check.\n\nAmong the checks available:\n- Q: a8-b7+ (check, limits king to d6)\n- Q: a8-c6+ (check, allows b8, d8, b7)\n- Q: a8-a7+ (check, allows b8, c8, d8, c6, b6, d6)\n\nSo **Q: a8-b7+** cuts off the most escape squares — only d6 possible.\n\nThus, it is the most forcing move.\n\nTherefore, I choose it.\n\nAnswer: Q: a8-b7"}
