 to d6, c5, etc., it will be restricted.\n- After Qe4+, Black must move (to f5 or d5), and then I can follow up with Kg3-f3 or Kf3-e3 to centralize.\n\nAlternatively, Qg6-f5+ immediately checks the king, forcing it to move to d6, d5, d4, e4, or f4 — but this may allow escape unless supported.\n\nHowever, **Qg6-e6** is even better: it attacks the entire 6th rank and forces the Black king backward. But wait — e6 is occupied by no one, and queen moves there freely.\n\nWait: Qg6-e6 is in the valid move list? Yes: "Q: g6-e6" is listed.\n\nBut let\'s compare:\n- Qg6-e6: threatens mate on d5 or f5 soon, and cuts off all escape to ranks 6–8.\n- Black king on e5 cannot go to e6, d6, f6, d5, f5 — already limited.\n- Then White king can march up: Kf3, Ke3, etc.\n\nBut actually, **Qg6-e4+** gives check now and gains tempo.\n\nLet’s see:\n\nAfter **Qg6-e4+**, Black king must move:\n- e5-d5 → Qd5# next? No, not yet.\n- e5-f5 → Qf5#\n- e5-d4 → Qd4#\n- e5-f4 → Qf4#\n\nWait! If Qe4+ and Black king is on e5, then after Qe4+, the king is in check — but **only if the queen attacks e5 from e4**?\n\nQueen on e4 attacks along the rank and file and diagonals.\n\nFrom e4, queen attacks:\n- e-file: e1–e8\n- 4th rank: a4–h4\n- Diagonals: h7-a0, b7-h1\n\nSo yes, **e4 attacks e5 vertically**!\n\nTherefore, **Qg6-e4+** is check!\n\nAnd the Black king on e5 is attacked by the queen on e4.\n\nSo Qg6-e4 is a **check**, and since the king is on e5, it is now under direct attack.\n\nLegal replies for Black:\n- e5-d5\n- e5-f5\n- e5-d4\n- e5-f4\n\nAll of these are poor, but none lead to immediate mate.\n\nBut this gains a crucial tempo.\n\nAlternatively, **Qg6-g7** or **Qg6-g8** just waste time.\n\nBest is to **cut off the king** first.\n\nStandard technique: use queen to create a "wall" one rank/file away, then push.\n\nCurrently, Black king is on e5 — central.\n\nBest practice: confine to edge using queen, then approach with king.\n\nSo moving queen to **e6**, **f6**, **d6**, etc., creates a barrier.\n\n**Qg6-e6** cuts off all squares on the 6th, 7th, 8th ranks for the Black king — very effective.\n\nAfter Qe6, Black king confined to e5, d5, f5, d4, e4, f4 — bottom-right quadrant.\n\nThen White king advances: Kf3, Ke3, etc.\n\nAlso, Qe6 does **not** give check, so Black cannot complain.\n\nBut it reduces mobility more than Qe4.\n\nCompare:\n\n- Qe6: limits Black king to 3 ranks (1-5) and files d-f\n- Qe4: gives check, forces king to react, but allows king to go to f5 or d5, possibly escaping central control\n\nActually, **Qe6 is safer and more restricting**.\n\nMoreover, after Qe6, if Black plays Kd5, then Qd6+ forces king back.\n\nSo Qe6 is better prophylactically.\n\nIs Qg6-e6 in the move list? Yes: "Q: g6-e6" is listed.\n\nAlso, king moves like Kg3-f3 are possible, but slower.\n\nReason: Using the queen to restrict the Black king’s space is the fastest path to checkmate. Qe6 creates a solid barrier on the 6th rank, limiting the Black king to the lower part of the board. This is a standard endgame technique. Bringing the king up after will lead to forced checkmate.\n\nAnswer: Q: g6-e6'}
