\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: c7-b6, K: c7-c6, K: c7-c8, K: c7-d6, K: c7-d7, K: c7-d8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: Only two kings remain on the board — White king at c7 and Black king at a8. The position is a king vs king endgame, which is inherently a draw because neither side can deliver checkmate with just a king. However, White has a significant spatial advantage, as the White king is more centralized and can restrict the Black king’s movement.\n\nEvaluation:  \n- K: c7-b6 — Moves closer to the corner, supporting potential restriction of Black king, but doesn’t make progress toward driving the Black king to the edge.\n- K: c7-c6 — Centralizes slightly, but less effective than b6 or d7.\n- K: c7-c8 — Moves directly toward the Black king, attacking it (check). This is a strong move as it forces the Black king to retreat (only legal move would be Kb8), reducing its space.\n- K: c7-d6 — Less central than other options, reduces coordination.\n- K: c7-d7 — Good centralization, controls d8 and e8, supports advancing further.\n- K: c7-d8 — Advances to the back rank, but may allow Black king to stay at a8 without immediate restriction.\n\nReason: The most active and forcing move is **K: c7-c8**, delivering check and immediately reducing the Black king's mobility. This is the best way to make progress, even though the game cannot be won, it avoids passive play and maximizes theoretical winning chances in case of opponent error (though impossible here due to insufficient material). It also positions the White king optimally for any future development (if more pieces were present).\n\nAnswer: K: c7-c8"}
