 position.\n\nGiven that the game state must be legal, the only possibility is that either:\n- The black king is *not* on b7, or\n- The white king is *not* on c8.\n\nBut per your input:\n- White king is on c8: KWF1\n- Black king is on b7: KBF1\n\nc8 and b7 are diagonally adjacent (distance of one square), so this is **illegal**.\n\nYet, since the problem presents this as the current state and offers valid moves including K: c8-c7, etc., we must assume the representation might have a typo or conceptual flaw.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the black king is not on b7 but on a different square? But you wrote:\n\n> 8 |    |    |KWF1|    |    |    |    |    | 8  \n> 7 |KBF1|    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 7\n\nThat places KBF1 (Black King) on a7, not b7.\n\nAh! Here's the key: the columns are labeled a-h, so:\n\n- Row 8: a8=empty, b8=empty, c8=KWF1 (White King), d8 to h8 empty\n- Row 7: a7=KBF1 (Black King), b7 to h7 empty\n\nSo the **white king is on c8**, the **black king is on a7**.\n\nNow compute distance:\n- a7 to c8: difference of 2 files (a→c), 1 rank (7→8) → knight move apart. Not adjacent. ✅ Legal.\n\nSo kings are **not** adjacent. Good.\n\nNow evaluate the valid moves for White:\n\nValid moves provided:\n- K: c8-c7\n- K: c8-d7\n- K: c8-d8\n\nLet’s assess legality and purpose:\n\n1. **K: c8-c7** — moves to c7. Distance from a7: a7 to c7 is two files apart, same rank → two squares apart horizontally. Not adjacent. c7 is adjacent to b7 and b6, etc. Is c7 adjacent to a7? No — a7 is two files away. So safe. Legal.\n\n2. **K: c8-d7** — d7 is two files right, one down from a7 → a7 to d7 is three files, same rank? a7→d7 is +3 files, 0 ranks → not adjacent. d7 is adjacent to c6, c7, c8, d6, d8, e6, e7, e8. a7 is far away. So safe. Legal.\n\n3. **K: c8-d8** — d8. a7 to d8: +3 files, +1 rank → not adjacent. Safe. Legal.\n\nNow, objective: White to move. Only kings left. **Checkmate is impossible**. Best outcome is **stalemate**, but only if Black has no legal moves.\n\nBut Black can move his king from a7 to: a8, b7, b8, b6, a6. Currently, after White’s move, we must avoid giving Black mobility.\n\nBut ultimately, **this is a dead draw** under insufficient mating material. However, if White can **stalemate** Black, it’s a draw.\n\nSo White should aim to restrict Black’s king.\n\nBlack king on a7. Possible moves: a8, b8, b7, b6, a6 — unless blocked.\n\nWhite king on c8. Can influence c7, d7, d8.\n\nBest plan: **cut off escape routes**.\n\nOption: **K: c8-c7** — controls b6, b7, b8, c6, c8, d6, d7, d8. In particular, c7 attacks b6, b7, b8, d6, d7, d8.\n\nFrom a7, Black wants to go to b7 or a8 or b8.\n\nIf White plays **K: c8-c7**, then:\n- b7 is attacked → Black cannot go to b7\n- a8 still available\n- b8 still available (a7→b8 is diagonal)\n- a6 available\n\nStill many moves.\n\nOption: **K: c8-d8** — moves to d8. Controls c7, c8, d7, e7, e8. From d8, does not attack b7 or b8. a7 can still go to b7, b8, a8, a6, b6.\n\nNo restriction.\n\nOption: **K: c8-d7** — moves to d7. Controls c6, c7, c8, d6, d8, e6, e7, e8.\n\nDoes it restrict Black? d7 is far from a7. Still, no direct pressure.\n\nWait — best way to restrict Black king is to occupy central squares and cut off retreat.\n\nBut here, the optimal strategy in king vs king endgame is to **oppose** the enemy king.\n\nCurrently, White king on c8, Black on a7 — file distance of 2 (a to c), rank difference 1 (7 to 8).\n\nTo make progress, White should try to gain opposition.\n\nBut with no pawns, no progress can be made.\n\nAll moves lead to a draw.\n\nBut among the options, **K: c8-c7** brings the king down to the 7th rank, directly threatening to dominate the center and potentially restrict b7.\n\nc7 is closer to the action than d8.\n\nSimilarly, **K: c8-d7** also approaches.\n\nBut **K: c8-c7** puts the king on the same rank as Black’s king (rank 7), file c vs a — two files apart.\n\nIt prepares to chase the king toward edge.\n\nFor example, after Kc8-c7, if Black plays Ka7-a8, then White can play Kc7-b6, cutting off.\n\nEventually, White may force the Black king to the corner.\n\nBut again, no pawns → cannot checkmate.\n\nSo all moves are equally drawn.\n\nBut if we must choose, **centralizing the king** is generally better.\n\nAmong the choices:\n- c7: central and active\n- d7: slightly more central\n- d8: passive, back rank\n\nd7 is more central than c7? d-file is more central than c.\n\nBut both are similar.\n\nActually, **K: c8-d7** moves toward the center of the board, while c7 is still on the flank.\n\nBut Black is on a7 — so chasing him requires going toward a-file.\n\nThus, **K: c8-c7** is better — it heads toward b-file.\n\nAfter c7, White can go to b6 or c6 to oppose.\n\nSo **K: c8-c7** is the most purposeful move.\n\nAlso, it avoids drifting to d-file, which gives Black freedom.\n\nThus, best practical choice.\n\nReason: Centralizing and preparing to restrict the Black king’s movement by approaching the a-file, maximizing coordination for potential opposition despite the inevitable draw.\n\nAnswer: K: c8-c7"}
